WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:02.260
Welcome to Math one oh two.

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:07.280
In Section five point three, we explore volumes by cylindrical shells.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.400
Let's see how changing the axis changes our best choice of slice.

4
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.560
Here's our region, under two x squared minus x cubed, from zero to two.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.520
Rotate this vertical strip around the x-axis.

6
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.120
See the disk?

7
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.140
Its radius reaches straight up to the curve.

8
00:00:23.677 --> 00:00:25.337
Now bring the region back flat.

9
00:00:25.997 --> 00:00:29.697
Change only the axis: this time, rotate around the y-axis.

10
00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:32.857
Same region, but look, a completely different solid.

11
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.520
Could we still use washers?

12
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:35.180
Yes.

13
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.680
Turn the strip sideways, perpendicular to the y-axis.

14
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.560
Rotate it, and a hole appears.

15
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.600
The left endpoint gives the inner radius; the right endpoint gives the outer radius.

16
00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.370
But now both radii change with height.

17
00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.170
We'd need two inverse branches of the cubic.

18
00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:55.710
That's possible, but the geometry is asking us to try another direction.

19
00:00:56.293 --> 00:00:57.893
Keep the strip vertical instead.

20
00:00:58.473 --> 00:01:00.033
It's parallel to the y-axis.

21
00:01:00.673 --> 00:01:04.433
As it turns, it wraps around the axis and becomes a cylindrical shell.

22
00:01:04.993 --> 00:01:07.493
Watch the gap inside: this is a thin wall.

23
00:01:07.943 --> 00:01:09.243
What dimensions do we need?

24
00:01:09.763 --> 00:01:12.063
The radius is x, measured from the axis.

25
00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:16.823
The height reaches up to the curve, so it's two x squared minus x cubed.

26
00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:18.483
The thickness is delta x.

27
00:01:18.977 --> 00:01:19.837
Let's open one up.

28
00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:22.657
Make a vertical cut and unroll just the middle surface.

29
00:01:23.457 --> 00:01:24.037
Now it's a rectangle!

30
00:01:24.037 --> 00:01:27.577
Its width is the distance once around: two pi r.

31
00:01:27.577 --> 00:01:29.117
Its height stays h.

32
00:01:29.437 --> 00:01:30.997
Keep the radial thickness separate.

33
00:01:31.477 --> 00:01:37.397
Circumference times height times thickness gives the volume of this constant-height shell, using its midpoint radius.

34
00:01:37.893 --> 00:01:39.713
Now nest several shells together.

35
00:01:40.313 --> 00:01:41.773
Each takes its height from the curve.

36
00:01:42.313 --> 00:01:43.693
See the steps along the top?

37
00:01:44.173 --> 00:01:46.553
Add more, thinner shells, and those steps shrink.

38
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:49.613
The collection follows the solid more and more closely.

39
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.750
Add all those small volumes.

40
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.550
As the thickness approaches zero, the sum becomes an integral.

41
00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:01.330
Radius times height is our key: x times two x squared minus x cubed.

42
00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.230
We'll leave the calculation for class.

43
00:02:03.843 --> 00:02:05.443
Let's watch both choices at once.

44
00:02:06.143 --> 00:02:07.503
On the left, the washer moves upward.

45
00:02:07.503 --> 00:02:12.323
Its inner radius grows and its outer radius shrinks, because both edges must meet the curve.

46
00:02:12.963 --> 00:02:14.283
On the right, the shell moves outward.

47
00:02:14.283 --> 00:02:17.563
Its radius grows, while its height first rises, then falls.

48
00:02:18.443 --> 00:02:20.523
Notice: each piece keeps following the same region.

49
00:02:21.163 --> 00:02:23.263
Two valid methods, two directions of slicing.

50
00:02:23.923 --> 00:02:25.583
Here, shells give us the simpler setup.

51
00:02:25.977 --> 00:02:27.637
Now you're ready for Example One.

52
00:02:28.197 --> 00:02:31.937
Identify the axis, choose the strip, then read its radius and height.

53
00:02:32.497 --> 00:02:33.337
Our integral is ready.

54
00:02:33.937 --> 00:02:35.217
That's our starting point for class.
